{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791436844,"nonce":"xVL1NllfYDUSWBAdJ3/4MXhSefY=","title":"HIR: remove dead arithmetic cycles after loop specialization","message":"The specialized float loop in Lua Collatz carries an integer shadow around its backedge: phi -\u003e multiply by 3 -\u003e add 1 -\u003e phi. Its remaining casts/comparisons are themselves unused. Final Cranelift code still emits the integer multiply/add and holds a register for this cycle. phi_prune treats every instruction operand as an observable read and only follows dependencies through phis, so the arithmetic cycle stays live.\n\nAdd conservative SSA liveness from observable operations and terminators through both instruction and phi definitions. Sweep unused total arithmetic and phis together; retain calls, memory operations, potentially trapping division/remainder/casts and unrecognized operations. Validate cyclic values, effects, traps, OSR and both language suites. Measure LLVM-off kernel and wall time; do not assume removing instructions guarantees a win.\n\nEvidence: /tmp/zyntax-collatz-next/fn-lua_main_1-body.hir and /tmp/zyntax-power2-div-normal-vcode.log. The specialized native loop still contains imulq $3 and leaq 1 for the integer shadow.","files":null}]}